    "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-LR-Project-Specialist-ON/606471117/": {
      "title": "LR Project Specialist",
      "description": "LR Project Specialist Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » LR Project Specialist Date Posted: 09/25/2026 Req ID: 50318 Faculty/Division: VP-People, Finance & Digital Services Department: Labour Relations Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: Under the general direction of the Assistant Director, Labour Relations, the LR Projects Specialist provides a broad range of project, reporting and administrative support to the Labour Relations team. The incumbent serves as the primary point of contact for divisional HR offices on a range of central reporting requirements and initiatives, including but not limited to, the USW seniority list, Redeployment Pool List, Bargaining Unit Review Committee notifications, and recurring reporting requirements to the various bargaining units. The incumbent investigates and resolves discrepancies arising out of the central reporting requirements, develops and produces complex data reports, making informed recommendations to Labour Relations staff regarding issues impacting their client groups. The incumbent coordinates and advances projects within the Labour Relations portfolio, including those involving data analytics, e.g. implementation of the new LR case management and reporting software. Using a strong understanding of the current issues in Labour Relations, the incumbent proactively identifies opportunities and makes recommendations to streamline reporting and tracking mechanisms. The incumbent develops and maintains effective working relationships with union representatives and communicates with them to edit and update collective agreements. QUALIFICATIONS REQUIRED I. EDUCATION: University degree or equivalent combination of education and experience. Completion of/or working toward CHRP designation or related designation preferred. Some experience in Labour Relations is an asset. II. EXPERIENCE: Minimum three years’ related experience in a Human Resources setting with exposure to labour relations. Experience with database design and implementation. Experience in analyzing datasets from multiple sources and providing data-driven consultations to stakeholders. Strong experience with research methods, business process analysis, and report writing. Experience leading or supporting data projects and initiatives. Experience working with collective agreements, HR policies, principles and practices. Knowledge of the University’s policies and practices as they pertain to faculty members, librarians, and staff. III. SKILLS: Advanced skills in database design and maintenance. Advanced MS Office skills. Advanced knowledge of HR systems (HRIS, ServiceNow, SuccessFactors). Exceptional analytical, problem-solving, and advanced research skills. IV: OTHER: Strong oral and written communication, interpersonal, analytical and problem-solving skills. Demonstrated organizational ability and attention to detail. Ability to take initiative. Demonstrated research and presentation skills. Knowledge of quantitative and qualitative statistical analysis procedures. Ability to work effectively with multiple and conflicting priorities, often under pressure, and to meet deadlines. Commitment to service delivery and collaboration. An awareness of and sensitivity to equity, diversity, and inclusion. Ability to maintain strict standards of confidentiality. Closing Date: 10/13/2026, 11:59PM ET Employee Group: Salaried Personnel Subarea: PM Appointment Type : Budget - Term Schedule: Full-Time Pay Scale Group & Hiring Zone: PM 2 -- Hiring Zone: $83,560 - $97,486 -- Broadband Salary Range: $83,560 - $139,264 Job Category: People, Finance & Digital Services Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively address and serve the interests of our global community. We strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples, Black and racialized persons, women, persons with disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities. We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Business Process, Administrative Assistant, HR, Statistics, Management, Human Resources, Administrative, Data, Research Apply now » Find similar jobs: Staff Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Assistant-Professor-Emerging-Directions-in-Chemical-Engineering-ON/606471517/": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor",
      "description": "Assistant Professor - Emerging Directions in Chemical Engineering Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Assistant Professor - Emerging Directions in Chemical Engineering Date Posted: 09/25/2026 Closing Date: 12/01/2026, 11:59PM ET Req ID: 50289 Job Category: Faculty - Tenure Stream (continuing) Faculty/Division: Faculty of Applied Science & Engineering Department: Department of Chemical Engineering and Applied Chemistry Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes POSITION DESCRIPTION The Department of Chemical Engineering & Applied Chemistry in the Faculty of Applied Science & Engineering at the University of Toronto invites applications for a full-time tenure stream position in the broad field of chemical engineering. The appointment will be at the rank of Assistant Professor, with an anticipated start date of July 1, 2027. This search aligns with the University’s commitment to strategically and proactively promote diversity among our community members ( Statement on Equity, Diversity & Excellence ). Recognizing that women, Black, Indigenous, and other Racialized communities have experienced inequities that have developed historically and are ongoing, we strongly welcome and encourage candidates from those communities to apply. We seek candidates pursuing innovative research in emerging and evolving areas of chemical engineering. Areas of interest include, but not limited to, sustainable and energy-efficient processes and industrial decarbonization; catalysis and reaction engineering; transport phenomena, including biotransport; advanced separations, including bioseparations; and food engineering and sustainable food processing. The Department is concurrently conducting focused faculty searches in Biological Engineering and Electrochemical Engineering. Candidates whose research is primarily aligned with either of these areas should apply to the corresponding focused searches titled Assistant Professor-Biological Engineering and Assistant Professor-Electrochemical Engineering on the University of Toronto faculty career opportunities website . REQUIREMENTS AND QUALIFICATIONS Candidates must have earned a PhD degree in Chemical Engineering or a related area by the time of appointment, or shortly thereafter, and must hold a baccalaureate degree in Chemical Engineering or an equivalent discipline, with a demonstrated record of excellence in research and teaching. We seek candidates whose research and teaching interests complement and enhance our existing departmental strengths . Candidates must provide evidence of research excellence which can be demonstrated through a record of publications in top-ranked and ﬁeld-relevant journals or forthcoming publications that meet high international standards; the submitted research statement; presentations at signiﬁcant conferences; awards and accolades; and strong endorsements from referees. Evidence of excellence in teaching will be demonstrated through teaching accomplishments, the teaching dossier (including the required materials outlined below, as well as evidence of superior performance in other teaching-related activities), and strong letters of reference. Other teaching-related activities can include performance as a teaching assistant or course instructor, experience leading successful workshops or seminars, student mentorship, or excellent conference presentations or posters. Equity and diversity are essential to academic excellence. We seek candidates who value diversity and whose teaching and service bear out our commitment to equity. Candidates are therefore also asked to submit a 1-2 page statement of contributions to equity and diversity, which might cover topics such as (but not limited to): teaching that incorporates a focus on underrepresented communities, the development of inclusive pedagogies, or the mentoring of students from underrepresented groups. RESPONSIBILITIES The successful candidate will be expected to pursue innovative and independent research, and to establish an outstanding, competitive, and externally funded research program. In addition, the successful candidate will be assigned a load contributing to the Department’s teaching and service activities. HIRING SALARY RANGE The expected salary range is $125,000-$160,000 CAD per annum, depending on experience. ABOUT US The Department of Chemical Engineering & Applied Chemistry at the University of Toronto is committed to its vision statement: “Through leading edge research and innovation, we integrate chemistry, biology and engineering to drive solutions to global challenges in energy, the environment and health”. For more information on the Department of Chemical Engineering & Applied Chemistry, please visit www.chem-eng.utoronto.ca . For more information on working at the University of Toronto, please visit https://www.utoronto.ca/research-innovation/working-at-uoft . APPLICATION PROCESS All application materials, including recent reference letters, must be received by 11:59 pm EST on December 1, 2026 . All qualiﬁed candidates are invited to apply online by clicking the link below. Your CV and cover letter should be uploaded into the dedicated ﬁelds. Please combine additional application materials into one PDF file. Applicants must submit the following materials: •    a cover letter (up to 2 pages); •    a current curriculum vitae; •    a research statement outlining current and future research interests (up to 5 pages, excluding references); •    three (3) sample publications; •    a teaching dossier, including: - a teaching statement, as detailed above (up to 2 pages), - sample course materials, teaching evaluations, and evidence of superior performance in other teaching-related activities, as applicable (1-8 pages); •    an equity and diversity statement, as detailed above (up to 2 pages); and •    the name and contact information of three references. The University of Toronto’s recruiting tool will automatically solicit and collect letters of reference from each referee within 48 hours of an application being submitted. Applicants remain responsible for ensuring that referees submit recent letters (on letterhead, dated and signed) by the closing date. Submission guidelines and details on the automatic reference letter collection, including timelines, are available in the candidate FAQ . If you have any questions about this position, please contact Professor Ramin Farnood at chair.chemeng@utoronto.ca . All qualified candidates are encouraged to apply; however, Canadians and permanent residents will be given priority. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively address and serve the interests of our global community. We strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples, Black and racialized persons, women, persons with disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities. We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to strengthening our academic mission. As part of your application, you will be asked to complete a brief Diversity Survey. This survey is voluntary. Any information directly related to you is confidential and cannot be accessed by search committees or human resources staff. Results will be aggregated for institutional planning purposes. For more information, please see http://uoft.me/UP . Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Chemical Engineer, Bioengineering, Biotech, Sustainability, Engineer, Engineering, Science, Energy Apply now » Find similar jobs: Faculty Opportunities, All Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"
    },
    "https://jobs.utoronto.ca/job/Toronto-Assistant-Associate-Professor-Academic-Orthopedic-Surgeon-ON/606468417/": {
      "title": "Assistant/ Associate Professor",
      "description": "Assistant/ Associate Professor - Academic Orthopedic Surgeon Job Details | University of Toronto Skip to main content Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities Faculty Opportunities Librarian Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Sign in as Casual / External / View Profile U of T Employee Sign In Home Current Opportunities All Opportunities Staff Opportunities USW Staff Appointed Opportunities Faculty Opportunities UTemp (Short-Term) Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Librarian Opportunities Student Opportunities Federated Colleges Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Opportunities CUPE 3902 (Unit 3) Emergency Posting Opportunities Applying to U of T Frequently Asked Questions Working at U of T People, Finance & Digital Services Workplace Inclusion Join Our Talent Network Search Keywords More Filter Options: Campus and Job Category Loading... Campus All Job Category All Clear Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Create Alert × Select how often (in days) to receive an alert: Apply now » Assistant/ Associate Professor - Academic Orthopedic Surgeon Date Posted: 09/25/2026 Closing Date: 10/30/2026 , 11:59PM ET Req ID: 50286 Job Category: Faculty - Clinical Faculty/Division: Temerty Faculty of Medicine Department: Dept of Surgery Hospital Affiliation: Unity Health Toronto - St. Michael's Campus: St. George (Downtown Toronto) Existing Vacancy: Yes Description: The Department of Surgery, Division of Orthopedic Surgery at St. Michael’s Hospital, Unity Health Toronto is inviting applications for the position of academic orthopedic surgeon at the rank of Assistant/ Associate Professor. The anticipated start date is Fall of 2027, with some flexibility. The successful candidate will join a dynamic nine-member division of orthopedic surgery and a multidisciplinary trauma program. It is expected that the chosen candidate will contribute to the continued growth, clinical excellence and innovation of the trauma and arthroplasty service at St. Michael’s Hospital. Evidence of excellence in teaching and research is a requirement. Applicants should demonstrate a strong record of clinical excellence, scholarly productivity, and commitment to teaching and mentorship. The ideal candidate will complement existing strengths within the St. Michael’s trauma and arthroplasty programs, and contribute to advancing evidence-based, patient-centered care, innovation and research excellence. We are seeking a surgeon-scientist/investigator orthopedic surgeon with fellowship training in both orthopedic trauma and total joint arthroplasty and young adult hip conditions. Specific expertise in acetabular trauma would be considered and asset. Additional expertise in advanced technologies including navigation and robotics is desirable. The candidate should have an advanced degree and dedicated academic training with aspirations to pursue the surgeon-scientist or surgeon-investigator career path. Candidates must hold certification in Orthopedic Surgery from the Royal College of Physicians and Surgeons of Canada or a recognized equivalent certifying body and must be eligible for licensure with the College of Physicians and Surgeons of Ontario. A graduate degree such as MSc / PhD or equivalent research training in any of the following areas: clinical epidemiology, basic science, health services research, education scholarship, clinical trials or quality improvement is strongly preferred. The Division of Orthopedic Surgery at St. Michael’s Hospital offers an exceptional academic environment within Unity Health Toronto, supported by strong interdisciplinary collaborations across neurology, trauma, critical care, and rehabilitation. The Division is part of a vibrant network of academic orthopedic surgery across the University of Toronto, with a longstanding tradition of excellence in clinical care, research, and education. St. Michael’s Hospital is committed to fostering a collegial, collaborative, and inclusive environment that prioritizes team-based care and patient-centered excellence. A high degree of professionalism and a commitment to clinical excellence and innovations in care are key features of the Department of Surgery at St. Michael’s Hospital and the chosen candidate should advance these attributes. Remuneration and rank as a member of the clinical faculty will be commensurate with qualifications and experience. Remuneration is in part related to clinical activity and within the usual range for an orthopaedic surgeon in the Province of Ontario, which exceeds $200,000 CAD per annum. Application instructions Interested applicants should submit: letter of interest, the name of three referees and curriculum vitae by November 01, 2026 to - Maricelle Bronilla: Maricelle.Bronilla@unityhealth.to . For more information about the Temerty Faculty of Medicine/Department of Surgery, please visit our home page at http://surgery.utoronto.ca . CAUTION: This ad is “posted only” to the U of T affiliated hospital job board. Please see the information directly above for the application instructions; applications submitted via the \"Apply Now\" button on this page will NOT be considered for this position. Diversity Statement The University of Toronto embraces Diversity and is building a culture of belonging that increases our capacity to effectively address and serve the interests of our global community. We strongly encourage applications from Indigenous Peoples, Black and racialized persons, women, persons with disabilities, and people of diverse sexual and gender identities. We value applicants who have demonstrated a commitment to equity, diversity and inclusion and recognize that diverse perspectives, experiences, and expertise are essential to strengthening our academic mission. Accessibility Statement The University strives to be an equitable and inclusive community, and proactively seeks to increase diversity among its community members. Our values regarding equity and diversity are linked with our unwavering commitment to excellence in the pursuit of our academic mission. The University is committed to the principles of the Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act (AODA). As such, we strive to make our recruitment, assessment and selection processes as accessible as possible and provide accommodations as required for applicants with disabilities. If you require any accommodations at any point during the application and hiring process, please contact uoft.careers@utoronto.ca . Job Segment: Orthopedic, Public Health, Rehabilitation, Epidemiology, Neurology, Healthcare Apply now » Find similar jobs: All Opportunities, Affiliated Hospital Opportunities Home Accessibility Land Acknowledgment utoronto.ca Opens in a new tab. Opens in a new tab. People, Finance & Digital Services"
    },
